{
  "table": {
    "name": "customers",
    "purpose": "One row per customer account (CustomerID is unique, 32,461 rows). The joins and sibling tables (gasstations, products, transactions_1k, yearmonth) indicate a fuel-retail / fleet-card business: customers are accounts that buy at gas stations, their individual purchases are in transactions_1k and their per-month aggregates in yearmonth, both keyed by CustomerID. Customers appear to be businesses rather than individuals, given the segment codes (SME/LAM/KAM). Each row records the account's billing currency and its market segment."
  },
  "columns": {
    "Currency": {
      "role": "code",
      "name": "billing_currency",
      "description": "ISO 4217 currency code of the customer's account/billing currency. Only two values, no nulls: 'CZK' (Czech koruna, 30,459 customers, ~93.8%) and 'EUR' (euro, 2,002 customers, ~6.2%). The EUR minority plausibly reflects customers billed in the eurozone (e.g. Slovakia), but the table itself gives no location evidence, so only the currency meaning is asserted.",
      "status": "resolved"
    },
    "CustomerID": {
      "role": "identifier",
      "name": "customer_id",
      "description": "Unique surrogate primary key identifying a customer account; one row per customer (distinct = row count, uniqueness 1.0). Integer values from 3 to 53,314 with gaps, consistent with an ID sequence after deletions. It is the join key for the rest of the database: transactions_1k.CustomerID (individual purchases) and yearmonth.CustomerID (monthly per-customer aggregates) both reference it many-to-one.",
      "status": "resolved"
    },
    "Segment": {
      "role": "code",
      "name": "customer_segment",
      "description": "Three-valued customer classification code, no nulls: 'SME' (26,763, ~82.5%), 'LAM' (3,658, ~11.3%), 'KAM' (2,040, ~6.3%). The most plausible reading is a B2B account tier by size/strategic importance: SME = small and medium enterprises, LAM = large accounts (large account management), KAM = key accounts (key account management); the heavily skewed distribution (mostly SME, few key accounts) fits a tiered customer base. An alternative reading that also fits the data is that the codes denote the sales coverage model, i.e. which kind of account manager (large account manager vs. key account manager vs. SME desk) owns the relationship, rather than the customer's size. There is no lookup table or label column to disambiguate the exact expansion, so the specific acronym meanings remain unverified.",
      "status": "tentative"
    }
  }
}
